- **Statement:** **Main Theorem 1.2:** hardness of factorials, truth of the \(\tau\)-conjecture, and the specified Taylor-approximation hardness statements each imply that the permanent lacks polynomial-size constant-free division-free circuits.
- **Equivalent useful direction:** Polynomial \(\tau(\mathrm{Per}_m)\) implies \(\tau(t!)=(\log t)^{O(1)}\); corresponding compressed Pochhammer and Taylor families follow through the paper’s general theorem.
- **Hypothesis audit:** The paper expressly warns that its permanent condition is not known equivalent to ordinary \(VP\ne VNP\). **Corollary 4.2** uses GRH for an implication involving arbitrary complex constants.
- **Primitive/relevance:** A precise conditional barrier to proving factorial or truncation hardness. It supplies no unconditional factoring algorithm or composite-ring prefix test. **VERIFIED.**

## 17. Cheng: short programs for multiples of factorials

**Source:** Qi Cheng, *On the Ultimate Complexity of Factorials*. Theoretical Computer Science 326 (2004), 419–429; 12-page author manuscript, internally undated. [Primary PDF](https://qcheng2023.github.io/paper/factorial.pdf).

