## 14. Lipton 2009: the factorial-GCD binary search

**Source:** Richard J. Lipton, *Factoring and Factorials*, 23 February 2009; author exposition. [Primary author article](https://rjlipton.com/2009/02/23/factoring-and-factorials/).

- **Problem/input:** Factor binary \(N\), given efficient straight-line factorial computations.
- **Statement:** **Theorem 1** asserts that \(O(\log^c t)\)-length straight-line computations for \(t!\) imply polynomial-size factoring circuits; the article separately identifies uniformity as necessary for a uniform conclusion.
- **Operational reduction:** Evaluate \(t!\bmod N\), compute \(\gcd(t!,N)\), and binary-search cutoffs. A proper GCD ends the search; otherwise the GCD is \(1\) or \(N\), determining the next interval.
- **Complexity:** \(O(\log N)\) factorial queries; each supplied division-free program can be evaluated modulo \(N\), avoiding enormous integer intermediates.
- **Primitive/relevance:** This is a close prior formulation of the brief’s prefix/factorial-unit strategy.
- **Qualification:** For the project, require a uniformly available factorial evaluator or explicitly bounded advice. Mere existence of unrelated short programs should not be silently promoted into an implementable uniform oracle. **VERIFIED author statement and reduction.**

## 15. Shub–Smale: factorial multiples and the \(\tau\)-conjecture

**Source:** Michael Shub, Steve Smale, *On the intractability of Hilbert’s Nullstellensatz and an algebraic version of “NP ≠ P?”*. Duke Math. J. 81 (1995), 47–54; readable manuscript dated 21 June 1994. [Author-hosted paper](https://profshub.github.io/articles/1995-Intractability_of_Hilberts_Nullstellensatz.pdf), [readable manuscript copy](https://janos.cs.technion.ac.il/COURSES/238900-13/Papers-1/ShubSmale-pap97.pdf).

- **Problem/representation:** Constant-free straight-line computation from \(1\), using \(+,-,\times\); arithmetic-operation complexity.
- **Statement:** The unnumbered **Main Theorem:** if factorials are not ultimately easy—there is no sequence of nonzero multiples \(m_t t!\) with polylogarithmic straight-line complexity—then Hilbert’s Nullstellensatz is intractable and \(P_{\mathbb C}\ne NP_{\mathbb C}\).
- **Intermediate theorem:** **Theorem 2:** a polylogarithmic BSS algorithm deciding whether \(z\in\{1,\ldots,t\}\) would make \(t!\) ultimately easy.
- **Conjecture:** The standard \(\tau\)-conjecture bounds the number of distinct integer roots of nonzero \(f\in\mathbb Z[X]\) by \((1+\tau(f))^C\).
- **Primitive/relevance:** Conditional complexity connections, not an evaluator. Exact factorials, arbitrary multiples, uniform algorithms, and arithmetic-operation bounds are different interfaces. **VERIFIED; standard conjecture formulation also checked in Bürgisser below.**

## 16. Bürgisser: factorial complexity versus permanent complexity

**Source:** Peter Bürgisser, *On Defining Integers And Proving Arithmetic Circuit Lower Bounds*. Computational Complexity 18 (2009), 81–103; author-uploaded manuscript. [Primary author upload](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/226946788_On_Defining_Integers_And_Proving_Arithmetic_Circuit_Lower_Bounds), [publisher record](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00037-009-0260-x).

