- **Status:** **VERIFIED.** The theorem improves a subroutine; it does not itself claim a new general factoring exponent.

## 32. Umans–Wang 2025: compressed divisibility covers

**Source:** Chris Umans, Siki Wang, *A number-theoretic conjecture implying faster algorithms for polynomial factorization and integer factorization*. arXiv:2511.10851v1, 13 November 2025. [Primary PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.10851v1).

- **Problem/input:** Factor binary \(N\) using a structured sequence \(A_1,\ldots,A_k\); put \(B=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor\).
- **Statement:** **Theorem 5.2:** if every integer at most \(B\) divides \(\prod A_i\), interval products modulo \(d\le N\) cost \(\widetilde O(\log d\,B^\gamma)\), and each \(A_i\)’s prime factors can be produced in \(\widetilde O(B^\gamma)\), then factoring costs
  \[
  \widetilde O(N^{\gamma/2}\log k).
  \]
- **Conditional improvement:** **Theorem 5.5** assumes the **Strong Prefactored \((\alpha,\beta)\)-Divisor Conjecture** and claims \(\widetilde O(N^{\max(\alpha,\beta)/2+o(1)})\). The optimal proposed \(\alpha=\beta=1/3\) gives exponent \(1/6\).
- **Hypotheses:** Structured sets \(S,T\), bounded-size differences \(s-t\), divisor coverage, and efficient factorization of those differences. Existence of a short covering product alone is insufficient.
- **Primitive/relevance:** Very close prior formulation of “compressed divisibility object plus efficient interval extraction.” **VERIFIED conditional statements; conjecture unresolved.**

## 33. Hittmeir 2023: Smooth Subsum Search

**Source:** Markus Hittmeir, *Smooth Subsum Search: A heuristic for practical integer factorization*. arXiv:2301.10529v2, 11 July 2023. [Primary text](https://arxiv.org/html/2301.10529v2).

- **Problem/input:** Binary \(N\), factor base, and modular roots of \(f(X)=(X+\lceil\sqrt N\rceil)^2-N\) modulo known small primes.
- **Result:** Sections 3–4 give an explicit heuristic smoothness-candidate generator and computational comparisons; no theorem there proves a new general factoring exponent.
- **Mechanism:** Chinese remaindering expresses candidates as subsums satisfying selected small-prime divisibility constraints. Search for small representatives relative to the selected modulus, then test residual smoothness and perform ordinary relation processing.
- **Hypotheses:** Practical smoothness behavior and parameter choices; modular roots concern known factor-base primes.
- **Complexity variables:** Factor-base sizes, selected subset size, candidate generation and filtering costs.
- **Primitive/relevance:** Produces candidates with deliberately imposed divisibility, offering an independent arithmetic-search mechanism. It does not compute the brief’s prefix predicate.
- **Status:** **VERIFIED as a heuristic algorithm; polynomial-time or improved asymptotic general factoring claim UNVERIFIED.**

## 34. Stănică–Mulder–Hittmeir 2026: exact sieve-set limitations

**Source:** Pantelimon Stănică, Erik Mulder, Markus Hittmeir, *Multi-target hyperbolic sieves and elliptic trace obstructions*. arXiv:2606.13018v2, 5 October 2026. [Primary text](https://arxiv.org/html/2606.13018v2).

- **Problem/input:** Known auxiliary odd prime \(\ell\nmid N\); sieve candidates for \(ap+q\bmod\ell\), where \(N=pq\).
- **Representation:**
  \[
  H_a(N;\ell)=\{ax+Nx^{-1}:x\in\mathbb F_\ell^\times\}.
  \]
- **Statements:** **Theorem 3.12:**
  \[
  K(A,cA)=-1-\chi(A)t_c,
  \]
  relating the relevant quartic character sum to an elliptic-curve trace. **Corollary 3.16:**
  \[
  \left||H_a\cup H_b|-3\ell/4\right|
  \le(2\sqrt\ell+7)/4.
  \]
- **Hypotheses:** Distinct nonzero \(a,b\); known field \(\mathbb F_\ell\). Stronger proposed uniform bounds fail in supplied counterexamples.
- **Complexity:** A local sieve-analysis result; no new complete-factorization time bound.
- **Primitive/relevance:** Explicit public residue filters, together with quantitative limits on batching several target forms. Useful for evaluating novelty of quotient/congruence sketches. **VERIFIED.**

## 35. Boneh–Durfee–Howgrave-Graham: large prime-power divisors

**Source:** Dan Boneh, Glenn Durfee, Nick Howgrave-Graham, *Factoring \(N=p^rq\) for Large \(r\)*. CRYPTO ’99, LNCS 1666, 326–337; author-uploaded full paper consulted. [Author record](https://crypto.stanford.edu/~dabo/pubs/abstracts/prq.html), [primary author upload](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/225776879_Factoring_N_p_r_q_for_Large_r).

- **Problem/input:** Binary \(N=p^rq\), supplied \(r,c\), with \(q<p^c\).
- **Statement:** **Theorem 2:** recover \(p\) deterministically in
  \[
  2^{\frac{c+1}{r+c}\log_2p}\,O(\gamma),
  \]
  where \(\gamma\) is LLL time for dimension \(O(r^2)\), entries of \(O(r\log N)\) bits; polynomial space.
- **Hint interface:** **Lemma 1:** an approximation \(P\) satisfying
  \[
  |P-p|<p^{1-c/(r+c)-2r/d}
  \]
  suffices using a dimension-\(d\) lattice.
- **Restricted polynomial-time family:** Fixed \(c\), \(r=\Theta(\log p)\).
- **Primitive/relevance:** Decode a coarse factor approximation when multiplicity is large; exhaustive guessing of the remaining required leading bits is explicitly charged.
- **Status:** **VERIFIED.** This does not cover ordinary balanced semiprimes, where \(r=1\).

## 36. Pollard \(p-1\): smooth order as sufficient partial structure

**Source:** J. M. Pollard, *Theorems on factorization and primality testing*. Proc. Cambridge Philos. Soc. 76 (1974), 521–528. [Primary publisher record](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/mathematical-proceedings-of-the-cambridge-philosophical-society/article/theorems-on-factorization-and-primality-testing/6762E84DBD34AEF13E6B1D1A8334A989).

- **Access status:** Publisher extract opened; full paper unavailable. Original numbered theorem and original cost statement **UNVERIFIED**.
- **Exact elementary interface:** For a known exponent \(E\) and unit \(a\bmod N\),
  \[
  p-1\mid E\Longrightarrow p\mid a^E-1.
  \]
  A factor follows if \(1<\gcd(a^E-1,N)<N\).
- **Restricted-family hypothesis:** Taking \(E=\operatorname{lcm}(1,\ldots,B)\) succeeds when the relevant prime-power divisors of \(p-1\) are covered and another prime component does not also become \(1\).
- **Derived cost:** Binary exponentiation costs \(O(\log E)\) modular multiplications; \(\log E=O(B)\), plus exponent construction and GCDs.
- **Primitive/relevance:** A known multiple of a hidden component order is usable information. Smoothness alone must be strong enough at the prime-power level; simultaneous annihilation of every component gives an unhelpful GCD \(N\).

## 37. May–Ritzenhofen: implicit shared-bit hints

**Source:** Alexander May, Maike Ritzenhofen, *Implicit Factoring: On Polynomial Time Factoring Given Only an Implicit Hint*. PKC 2009; full proceedings paper. [Primary PDF](https://www.iacr.org/archive/pkc2009/54430001/54430001.pdf).

- **Problem/input:** Two different RSA moduli \(N_i=p_iq_i\); promise that \(p_1,p_2\) share \(t\) least significant bits. The common bits themselves are not supplied.
- **Statement:** **Theorem 5:** if \(q_i\) have \(\alpha\) bits and
  \[
  t>2(\alpha+1),
  \]
  both moduli can be factored in quadratic time.
- **Representation/hypotheses:** Odd RSA factors permit inversion modulo \(2^t\). A two-dimensional lattice contains \((q_1,q_2)\); Gauss reduction and the stronger inequality give the rigorous result.
- **Multiple-modulus qualification:** **Theorem 7** uses \(t\ge k\alpha/(k-1)\), but depends on **Assumption 6** identifying the desired shortest vector; it is not unconditional.
- **Primitive/relevance:** A concrete correlated-information format that suffices without revealing the common bit string. The rigorous two-modulus regime is substantially unbalanced; do not transfer it to two ordinary balanced RSA moduli. **VERIFIED.**

## 38. Sica: “factoring with hints” can require exponentially many hints

**Source:** Francesco Sica, *Factoring with Hints*. arXiv:0912.1585v6, last revised 8 August 2017; downloaded PDF internally dated 26 October 2018. [Record](https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.1585v6), [primary PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0912.1585v6).

- **Problem/input:** Binary semiprime \(N=pq\), plus complete factorizations of integers near \(N\).
- **Statement:** **Theorem 1:** for arbitrary \(\epsilon>0\), factorizations of \(O(N^{1/3+\epsilon})\) nearest integers suffice to recover \(p,q\) in \(O(N^{1/3+\epsilon})\) bit operations.
- **Hypotheses/representation:** Explicit neighboring-integer factorizations; their acquisition is external to the recovery theorem.
- **Complexity variables:** Numeric \(N\), number of hints, and bit-operation recovery cost. This is exponential in the original bit length \(n\).
- **Primitive/relevance:** A downstream decoder for a precisely specified arithmetic hint collection. It does not justify treating a short description of neighboring factorizations as access to those factorizations.
- **Status:** **VERIFIED theorem; the record/PDF date discrepancy is preserved rather than resolved.**

## 39. Consequences for the project’s exact interfaces

These are deductions from the verified interfaces above, not literature priority claims.

- **Binary sections:** If the sections selected along a path can be **uniformly constructed as polynomial-size division-free circuits over \(R_N\)**, Agrawal–Biswas supplies the needed randomized formal zero tests. Amplify across \(O(n)\) adaptive decisions. Thus compact, efficient section construction is a sufficient remaining primitive; a separate new composite-ring PIT theorem is unnecessary.
- **Order of vanishing:** For degree bound \(D\), reversal gives
  \[
  \operatorname{ord}_0P=D-\deg(X^DP(X^{-1})).
  \]
  Gatewise reversal with syntactic degree bounds yields a polynomial-size division-free circuit, including over composite rings. This connects valuation queries to semantic degree queries, but supplies no efficient degree algorithm.
- **Factorial prefixes:** BGS already solves the exact bounded-divisor task with square-root dependence on cutoff length. Lipton and Umans–Wang already formulate extraction from compressed divisibility products.
- **Novelty boundary:** A substantive advance would be an efficient new section/truncation representation, a cheaper exact unit predicate, or an efficiently produced hint matching a verified decoder—not another equivalence between factorial prefixes and factoring.

